WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.440
Secure file handling protects data and systems when applications receive, store, process, and return files.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.660
Files can carry user-controlled names, metadata, content, paths, formats, and sizes.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:20.160
They can also represent evidence, exports, reports, backups, documents, and integration data.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:27.100
Because files move across trust boundaries, they deserve explicit security design rather than casual storage and retrieval.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.480
Strong practice starts with validation.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:38.420
Treat names, paths, extensions, content type claims, sizes, metadata, and formats as untrusted until checked.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:49.920
Use safe server-controlled storage names, controlled paths, least-privilege permissions, tenant-aware metadata, and clear separation between intake, quarantine, processing, and publication.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.100
Keep sensitive uploads away from direct public access unless publication is intentional.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.360
Download and processing workflows need the same care.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:10.100
Authorize every protected download, prefer indirect references, use signed URLs thoughtfully, set safe Content-Disposition and Content-Type behavior, and review exports for excessive data exposure.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.480
Process files defensively with limits, parser hygiene, temporary directory cleanup, and safe logging.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.900
Scanning and sandboxing can add useful layers when appropriate.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:29.480
The final goal is to keep files from becoming a path to unauthorized access, data exposure, unsafe execution, resource exhaustion, or operational failure.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:37.860
File-handling systems should be observable, monitored, retained according to policy, deleted when appropriate, and ready for incident response.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.960
A file workflow is secure when it is validated, controlled, authorized, monitored, and cleaned up from end to end.
